## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`). You must switch to cooperative cancellation.

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` or `Join(timeout)` |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to loop condition check
                // Loop will exit because token.IsCancellationRequested is true
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive, there's nothing equivalent to Abort().
        // The thread MUST cooperate. See pitfalls below.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

Your `GetNextItem()` likely blocks (e.g., on a queue). You **must** make it cancellation-aware, or the thread won't wake up:

```csharp
// Before: may block indefinitely
private Item GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when token fires
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
```

If using `BlockingCollection<T>`, it already accepts a `CancellationToken`. If using custom blocking (e.g., `WaitOne`), replace with:

```csharp
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _dataReady, token.WaitHandle });
token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `Stop()` hangs forever — the thread never wakes up to check cancellation |
| **Forgetting `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem`** | Long-running items delay shutdown indefinitely |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Timer/callback leaks if `CancelAfter` is used |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated cancellations |
| **Assuming cancellation is immediate** | It's cooperative — only fires at the next checkpoint. Add checks between expensive operations |
| **No fallback for truly stuck threads** | If code can't cooperate (e.g., native call), move to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` |

---

### If You Need a Hard Timeout (Replaces the Abort Fallback)

```csharp
public void Stop(TimeSpan hardTimeout)
{
    _cts?.Cancel();

    if (_workerThread?.Join(hardTimeout) == false)
    {
        // Thread is stuck in uncooperative code.
        // Log a warning — there is NO safe way to force-kill a managed thread.
        Log.Warning("Worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        // Options: abandon it (IsBackground=true means it dies at process exit),
        // or redesign the blocking work into a child process.
    }
}
```

The fundamental truth: **there is no .NET 8 equivalent of `Thread.Abort()`**. Your code *must* cooperate with cancellation at every blocking point.
